  "mother": {
    "preparation": {
      "model_calls": 1,
      "phase": "preparation",
      "plan": {
        "A": "Develop a low-overhead dense DP baseline: use one in-place row, replacing modulo division with conditional subtraction because both summands are below MOD. Eliminate row copying and investigate compact obstacle storage versus sorted per-row obstacle lists, avoiding quadratic obstacle allocation where practical. Enable fast standard input handling. Preserve blocked-cell resets and initialization, including n=1, duplicate obstacles, and blocked endpoints. Measure complete execution, including parsing and allocation.",
        "B": "Research an exact sparse-obstacle algorithm. Sort and deduplicate obstacles, precompute factorials and inverse factorials modulo 1000000007 through 2n-2, and count monotone paths using binomial coefficients. For each obstacle in coordinate order, subtract paths passing through earlier coordinate-wise reachable obstacles; apply the same recurrence to the destination. This gives O(n+m²) time and O(n+m) memory after deduplication. Handle blocked endpoints explicitly and obtain the empty-obstacle result directly from the same formula. Compare against dense DP and propose a measured, input-size-based crossover; retain a general fallback rather than assuming obstacles are always few.",
        "C": "Research ARM64 four-core acceleration of dense DP without violating its left/up dependencies. Investigate tiled wavefront execution: tiles on the same tile anti-diagonal are independent once their top and left boundaries are available, while each tile performs an efficient sequential local recurrence. Keep boundary storage compact rather than requiring a full integer grid. Explore tile sizes, scheduling overhead, and a serial threshold under the existing compilation environment. An alternative is a row-wise segmented prefix scan with blocked cells resetting the sum; account for synchronization and extra passes before pursuing it. Benchmark end-to-end against optimized serial DP, not just the original.",
        "G": "Integrate only correct, measured programs from A/B/C. First retain the strongest standalone candidate, then evaluate combinations and a dispatcher based on actual n and deduplicated obstacle count, never test identities or expected outputs. Share parsing and obstacle normalization only when their overhead is justified. Measure each integrated candidate independently: sparse preprocessing, parallel startup, cache behavior, and dispatch can erase individual gains. Preserve a general dense fallback and exact modular semantics. Use the unchanged official validation and measurement process; report timings and retain the best verified implementation without changing compiler, target, tests, oracle, or budgets.",
        "analysis": "The supplied source implements Problem D, not the other problems in the document. Its quadratic DP uses a quadratic bit-packed obstacle grid, two integer rows, per-cell remainder operations, and a full row copy. The recurrence needs only linear DP storage, and adding two reduced residues safely fits signed 32-bit arithmetic. The only measured implementation is the correct baseline: four validation cases and three timings averaging 0.081483 seconds. No input dimensions or obstacle distributions are reported, so neither sparse-method superiority nor parallel speedup is established. The target has four ARM64 CPU cores and no GPU; this short measured runtime makes overhead especially important. A targets constant factors, B targets algorithmic complexity for sparse inputs, and C targets dependency-safe parallelism. All paths must remain correct for every valid input."
      },
      "request_sha256": "eabee27b7844aa59d24b428ebcf04d2cd89d6015abc5f70f5bd716989c6284a9",
